<%#
  Turf Monster's own section of the living style guide.

  THE POINT OF THIS FILE, in one sentence: every card below opens the REAL
  production modal, so there is no second copy to drift from the one players
  meet. Until studio-engine 0.71.0 the only way to get an app's card onto the
  guide was to write a mirror of it under the gem's style/modals/, and five of
  turf's cards had one. Those mirrors drifted — wallet_setup's dropped the whole
  isMobile axis its real card forks on three times, and wallet_changed's opened
  with a `currentAddress` prop the real card has never read. This section
  replaced them, and /tasks/retire-five-modal-mirrors then deleted those five
  from the gem once the prose below had landed here. That is the ORDER this
  file's cards arrive in, and it is deliberate: transplant the prose first,
  delete the mirror second, because the prose is the only part of a mirror
  worth keeping and a deletion takes it with everything else.

  WHERE EACH BATCH'S MIRROR STANDS TODAY. The second half of that order — the
  deletion — is recorded HERE rather than in a per-batch preamble, because that
  is where batch 1's was recorded and there is no batch-1 preamble to hold it.
  Measured against the RESOLVED gem, studio-engine 0.74.4: batch 1's five are
  gone (above), batch 2's five are gone too — studio-engine's
  retire-five-more-mirrors deleted style/modals/_quest_success,
  _ds_newsletter_success, _unsubscribe_goodbye, _wallet_deposit and
  _network_guard — and batch 3's five are STILL STANDING, so batch 3's preamble
  holds the only undischarged hedge in this file.

  THIS PARAGRAPH IS THE ONE CLAIM HERE A GEM BUMP CAN FALSIFY, which is why it
  names the version it was measured at instead of speaking in an eternal present
  tense. The consequence that actually matters — WHICH of this section's ids the
  gem still registers under the same string — is not written down anywhere in
  this file at all: style_host_section_test derives it from the rendered page on
  every run, so the next retirement reddens a test instead of quietly rotting a
  comment. That is the whole lesson of the batch-2 preamble below, which said
  "still standing" for as long as it took someone to read it again.

  WHAT THE GEM OWNS AND WHAT THIS FILE OWNS. The section element, its anchor,
  the heading and the sticky-nav pill are all rendered by studio-engine's
  style/_host.html.erb. This file supplies cards and nothing else — no <section>,
  no <h2>, no nav entry. Defining the file IS the registration; the guide finds
  it by convention with lookup_context.exists?("modals", ["style/host"], true).

  EVERY TRIGGER DRIVES $store.modals, NOT dsModals — and that is the whole
  mechanism, not a style preference. dsModals is the page-scoped host the gem's
  own Modals section stands up, because ITS specimens carry engine ids that no
  app layout registers. Turf's ids ARE registered in turf's layout host (this app
  mounts studio/modals/host), so pushing one onto the real store makes the real
  host render the real card. Copy dsModals in here and you have rebuilt the
  mirror this section exists to delete.

  PROPS COME FROM THE CARD, NEVER FROM A SPECIMEN. Each open_expr below passes
  only keys the production partial actually reads, verified against the partial
  on 2026-09-08. The retired mirrors are the cautionary tale twice over: one
  opened wallet-changed with `currentAddress` (the card reads oldAddress), and
  one offered wallet-setup a `detected` toggle that exists only on the mirror —
  the real card decides that itself from a runtime phantomPresent getter that no
  prop can set. That is rule 3 of the modal lifecycle SOP, failing on click
  instead of in review.
%>

<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-4">

  <%= render layout: "style/modal_specimen", locals: {
        label:     "Set up your wallet",
        reference: %(turf-monster's modals/_wallet_setup — how an account with NO wallet gets one, and the third step of the post-auth onboarding chain (it renders a 3-of-3 pill, which is the chain's position, not this section's). AN APP-SPECIFIC FLOW: the engine lends only chrome — blocks/_shell, blocks/_progress_pill, blocks/_wallet_brand_sprite — while extension detection, the 90-second walkthrough video, the Detailed Guide route and the managed-wallet fallback all stay here, which is why the card is ~627 lines. IT IS THE OPPOSITE POPULATION to the Sign Wallet cards in the engine's Web3 section: WalletSetupPolicy exits at its own step 1 for an account that already holds a wallet, so no user ever meets both. THE INSTALL-VERSUS-INSTALLED ROW IS NOT A PROP — the card reads a live phantomPresent getter, so what you see here is what THIS browser can detect. The retired mirror offered a "Wallet detected" toggle instead, which is precisely the kind of convenience a mirror can afford and a real card cannot.),
        open_expr: "$store.modals.open('wallet-setup', { returnUrl: window.location.pathname })",
        openable:  true } do %>
    <div class="pointer-events-none w-40 rounded-lg bg-surface border border-subtle shadow p-4 text-center space-y-2">
      <div class="text-2xl leading-none">&#128091;</div>
      <span class="block h-2 w-24 mx-auto rounded" style="background: var(--color-text); opacity: .18"></span>
      <span class="block h-8 w-full rounded" style="background: var(--color-text); opacity: .10"></span>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <%= render layout: "style/modal_specimen", locals: {
        label:     "Wallet changed",
        reference: %(turf-monster's modals/_wallet_changed — fired when the person switches accounts INSIDE their wallet extension while signed in. Nothing is broken, and that is what makes it delicate: the session and the extension now disagree about who this is, and every on-chain action from here would use an identity the page is not showing. So it prints BOTH addresses, truncated the Solana way, because recognising your own is the one check the app cannot make for you. Continuing re-derives a session against the new wallet, which is a round trip, so the button carries a busy state rather than looking dead. THE PROP NAMES MATTER: the card reads oldAddress, newAddress and providerLabel. The retired mirror opened it with `currentAddress`, which the card has never read — a prop name that drifted and that no test caught, because a mirror answers to nobody.),
        open_expr: "$store.modals.open('wallet-changed', { oldAddress: '7xKpDemoSignedInWallet9JZ2Q', newAddress: '9mQtDemoSwitchedWalletLd4V', providerLabel: 'Phantom' })",
        openable:  true } do %>
    <div class="pointer-events-none w-40 rounded-lg bg-surface border border-subtle shadow p-4 text-center space-y-2">
      <div class="text-2xl leading-none">&#128260;</div>
      <span class="block h-1.5 w-28 mx-auto rounded" style="background: var(--color-text); opacity: .3"></span>
      <span class="block h-1.5 w-28 mx-auto rounded" style="background: var(--color-text); opacity: .3"></span>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <%= render layout: "style/modal_specimen", locals: {
        label:     "Buy an Entry Token",
        reference: %(turf-monster's modals/_buy_entry_token — reached when a player has picked a lineup and has no way to pay for it, which is why the copy says the lineup is SAVED: losing their picks is the one thing they are afraid of at that moment. Built from the engine's blocks/_close_x and blocks/_rail_row. THE RANKING IS A PRODUCT DECISION, not a visual one: Coinflow leads and carries the "New" badge because it mints a spendable entry token directly, the shortest path from here to being in the contest, while the Stripe rail swaps into the auth wizard's tokens picker — the older and longer path — and so defers. EACH RAIL SELF-GATES on onramp_rail_visible?, so on a stack with both flags off this card can render with no rails at all; that empty state is real and is tracked separately.),
        open_expr: "$store.modals.open('buy-entry-token', {})",
        openable:  true } do %>
    <div class="pointer-events-none w-40 rounded-lg bg-surface border border-subtle shadow p-4 space-y-2">
      <span class="block h-6 w-full rounded" style="background: var(--color-primary); opacity: .35"></span>
      <span class="block h-6 w-full rounded" style="background: var(--color-text); opacity: .12"></span>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <%# CAPABILITY-GATED, and the card says so rather than lying. cdp-ramp is
      registered in layouts/application behind cdp_ramp_modal_available?
      (logged_in? && AppFlags.cdp_ramp?), and ENABLE_CDP_RAMP is off by default
      everywhere — so on a stack without it this id has NO registration and a
      trigger would open an empty panel. Greying it is the honest state: the flag
      is ON wherever the flow actually exists (QA and production both set it), so
      the card opens the real thing exactly where the real thing is real.
      Deliberately NOT registering it ungated for the guide's benefit: that would
      ship the modal's markup into every authenticated page of an app that has no
      Coinbase ramp, to make one admin card clickable. %>
  <%= render layout: "style/modal_specimen", locals: {
        label:          "Coinbase ramp — buy preflight",
        reference:      %(turf-monster's modals/_cdp_ramp, opened at its FIRST step of the BUY flow. DELIBERATELY PARTIAL, and saying so is the point: that modal is a two-flow step machine — buy and cash-out — across NINE distinct steps rendered by TWELVE template arms (preflight, opening, waiting, awaiting-cdp, send, settling, done, failed, error), and this cards exactly one of them. The buy preflight is the state both funding cards hand off to, and therefore the only one a player reaches without already being mid-flow. The rest are uncarded BY DECISION (operator, 2026-08-26), which is a different thing from an oversight — a cabinet that shows one state of a multi-state machine without admitting it teaches a reader that the machine is simpler than it is. BOTH KEYS ARE REQUIRED: the flow arm is chosen by an isBuy getter in shared/_alpine_factories that reads props.flow === 'buy' strictly, with no default, so a step passed without a flow silently renders the CASH-OUT arm. Drive the others by varying step.),
        open_expr:      "$store.modals.open('cdp-ramp', { flow: 'buy', step: 'preflight' })",
        disabled:       !cdp_ramp_modal_available?,
        disabled_label: "requires ENABLE_CDP_RAMP",
        openable:       cdp_ramp_modal_available? } do %>
    <div class="pointer-events-none w-40 rounded-lg bg-surface border border-subtle shadow p-4 text-center space-y-2">
      <div class="text-2xl leading-none">&#127974;</div>
      <span class="block h-1.5 w-24 mx-auto rounded" style="background: var(--color-text); opacity: .3"></span>
      <span class="block h-6 w-full rounded" style="background: var(--color-primary); opacity: .3"></span>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <%= render layout: "style/modal_specimen", locals: {
        label:     "Co-sign refused",
        reference: %(turf-monster's modals/_cosign_rejected, opened when POST confirm_onchain_entry answers 422 with code tx_rejected: the SERVER refused to co-sign because the transaction it was handed did not match the entry it had prepared. A DIFFERENT KIND OF FAILURE from an on-chain error — that is the chain saying no, this is the server saying no. APP-SPECIFIC: the engine owns no entry preparation, no co-signing and no verdict; it lends blocks/_card_header and nothing else. THE COPY IS DELIBERATELY VAGUE AND SHOULD STAY THAT WAY: good actors never see this card, since it fires on a tampered or mismatched transaction, so naming WHICH server-side check failed would help an attacker probe the validation. What it does commit to is the part the one honest user needs — nothing was submitted, the funds are safe. Registered app-wide through modals/_host_extras rather than a layout list, so it is reachable on every path through the host.),
        open_expr: "$store.modals.open('cosign-rejected', {})",
        openable:  true } do %>
    <div class="pointer-events-none w-40 rounded-lg bg-surface border border-subtle shadow p-4 text-center space-y-2">
      <div class="text-2xl leading-none">&#128737;&#65039;</div>
      <span class="block h-1.5 w-28 mx-auto rounded" style="background: var(--color-text); opacity: .3"></span>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <%# ─── batch 2, the cheap five (2026-09-09) ───────────────────────────────
      Five more cards whose engine mirrors carried nothing this app disagrees
      with, or one fragment each. Their reference prose was transplanted here
      from style/modals/_quest_success, _ds_newsletter_success,
      _unsubscribe_goodbye, _wallet_deposit and _network_guard. DISCHARGED:
      studio-engine's retire-five-more-mirrors then deleted all five, in the
      same order batch 1 used, so nothing was dropped in the gap. The header's
      batch ledger carries the gem version that was measured; this preamble
      deliberately carries no second copy of it.

      THESE FIVE IDS NO LONGER COLLIDE with the gem's own Modals section on this
      page: its four same-string specimens went with the mirrors above, and
      ds-newsletter-success never collided at all — the gem spelled it with the
      ds- prefix. The collision that still makes the section test filter
      registrations down to this app's host is batch 3's, below. NO COUNT LIVES
      IN THIS FILE, and that is the correction this preamble is carrying: it
      used to say FOUR OF THESE FIVE, which was true for about a day. The test
      derives the colliding set from the rendered page instead. %>

  <%# THE PROPS THE REAL CALLER PASSES ARE NOT THE PROPS THIS CARD READS, which
      is why the trigger below passes none. Measured 2026-09-09:
      shared/_alpine_factories swaps to this id with seeds_earned, seeds_total
      and seeds_level, and modals/_quest_success reads NONE of the three — its
      x-data is empty and its bar comes from the server-side display_seeds_data
      helper. Passing them would render an identical card while teaching a
      reader three prop names the card has never answered to. The retired mirror
      drifted the other way, inventing a subscribed toggle and a seedsTotal that
      exist nowhere in this app. %>
  <%= render layout: "style/modal_specimen", locals: {
        label:     "Quest success (off-page)",
        reference: %(turf-monster's modals/_quest_success — the off-page quest celebration, shown when a quest completes away from any quest card. THE SAME BEAT AS THE LEVELING CELEBRATION, reached from somewhere else: that one fires INSIDE the modal that did the work, while this is what the host shows when there was no quest card to advance in place — a /account username change — so the celebration has to arrive on its own. The engine lends blocks/_card_header; the seeds bar is this app's components/_seeds_bar, fed display_seeds_data — which returns NIL on a cold cache, not a zero payload (application_controller reads the cache only; its seeds_payload(0) branch is the guest/non-wallet one). The zero payload is the BAR's own `seeds_data ||=` default, and the bar then self-heals from the seedsNavbar value StateFanout just bumped. THE FORK AT THE FOOT is why this is one card and not two: a viewer who has NOT joined the newsletter is offered it as the next quest, so the celebration doubles as the funnel, and one who already has is sent to the contest where the remaining quests live. That gate is SERVER-rendered from current_user.subscribed_to_newsletter?, precisely so the CTA never flashes the wrong option at someone already in — which is also why this card carries no toggle and should not grow one. IT OPENS WITH NO PROPS BY MEASUREMENT rather than by omission: the real swap in shared/_alpine_factories passes seeds_earned, seeds_total and seeds_level, and the partial reads not one of them.),
        open_expr: "$store.modals.open('quest-success', {})",
        openable:  true } do %>
    <div class="pointer-events-none w-40 rounded-lg bg-surface border border-subtle shadow p-4 text-center space-y-2">
      <div class="text-2xl leading-none">&#127793;</div>
      <span class="block h-2 w-24 mx-auto rounded" style="background: var(--color-text); opacity: .18"></span>
      <%# the seeds bar, which is this card's middle %>
      <span class="flex gap-1">
        <span class="flex-1 h-2 rounded" style="background: var(--color-primary)"></span>
        <span class="flex-1 h-2 rounded" style="background: var(--color-primary); opacity: .4"></span>
        <span class="flex-1 h-2 rounded" style="background: var(--color-primary); opacity: .2"></span>
      </span>
      <span class="block h-5 w-full rounded" style="background: var(--color-primary); opacity: .35"></span>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <%= render layout: "style/modal_specimen", locals: {
        label:     "Subscribed!",
        reference: %(turf-monster's modals/_newsletter_success — the standalone newsletter celebration. ITS ONLY CALL SITE IS A SWAP, measured 2026-09-09: modals/_newsletter_subscribe swaps to it on a successful join, and the /account newsletter row leads there by opening newsletter-subscribe — nothing opens this card directly. It still has to carry its own way forward, because it arrives on its own; that is what separates it from the gem's join-newsletter leveling beat, which celebrates IN PLACE on the card the person was already standing on. NOTE THE ID IS newsletter-success: the retired mirror was filed as _ds_newsletter_success and registered as ds-newsletter-success. The gem recorded no reason for the rename and its ds- prefix was never a rule — the four turf ports it carried alongside this one (quest-success, unsubscribe-goodbye, wallet-deposit, network-guard) took no prefix, and all five of those specimens have since been retired from the gem, _ds_newsletter_success included. Measured at studio-engine 0.74.4: the gem registers neither newsletter-success nor ds-newsletter-success, so the plainer name has nothing here to collide with. Nothing in this app answers to the ds- form. TWO FORKS, AND NEITHER IS VISIBLE TO A READER OF THE MARKUP. THE SEEDS BAR IS FIRST-JOIN ONLY — a re-subscribe earns nothing, and the card HIDES the bar rather than showing a flat zero, which would read to the person as a bug about their own account rather than as an accurate zero. THE CTA IS SERVER-DECIDED — with the username quest still open, meaning current_user.next_quest is :username, the button nudges straight into it, and otherwise it sends them off to browse contests; showing only one of those hides the funnel this celebration doubles as. ONLY THE FIRST FORK IS A PROP. The mirror made both into toggles so its cabinet could walk four combinations, and the real card reads exactly one key, props.seeds_earned, gating the bar on it being above zero. The CTA fork is server-rendered, precisely so the button never flashes the wrong option at someone whose quest is already done, and no prop can move it.),
        open_expr: "$store.modals.open('newsletter-success', { seeds_earned: 25 })",
        openable:  true } do %>
    <div class="pointer-events-none w-40 rounded-lg bg-surface border border-subtle shadow p-4 text-center space-y-2">
      <div class="text-2xl leading-none">&#127793;</div>
      <span class="block h-2 w-20 mx-auto rounded" style="background: var(--color-text); opacity: .18"></span>
      <%# the first-join seeds bar, hidden entirely on a re-subscribe %>
      <span class="flex gap-1">
        <span class="flex-1 h-2 rounded" style="background: var(--color-primary)"></span>
        <span class="flex-1 h-2 rounded" style="background: var(--color-primary); opacity: .35"></span>
        <span class="flex-1 h-2 rounded" style="background: var(--color-primary); opacity: .18"></span>
      </span>
      <span class="block h-5 w-full rounded" style="background: var(--color-primary); opacity: .35"></span>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <%= render layout: "style/modal_specimen", locals: {
        label:     "See you later",
        reference: %(turf-monster's modals/_unsubscribe_goodbye — the final beat after a confirmed unsubscribe, swapped in by modals/_unsubscribe_confirm. The smallest card in this section, and it earns its place: it is the half of the newsletter story a guide most easily forgets. The way IN is carded twice over, subscribe and Subscribed!, and a section that only ever demonstrates opt-in is not showing the flow, it is showing the happy half of it. APP-SPECIFIC, engine chrome only, and the reason is narrower than it used to be stated. THE ENGINE DOES OWN A NEWSLETTER — Studio::Newsletter, the DELETE profile/newsletter route drawn at lib/studio.rb:962, and its own /profile row — and what it does NOT own is this beat: measured at studio-engine 0.74.4, studio/profiles/_newsletter_modals registers exactly two ids, newsletter-unsubscribe and newsletter-email, and there is no goodbye among them. The engine's leave ends at the confirmation; this card is what turf shows after it. It lends blocks/_card_header and the tone. THE COPY IS NOT A GUILT TRIP: change your mind anytime from your account is a door left open rather than a plea, and it is worth preserving if this is ever reworded. It reads no props at all, which is the one thing the retired mirror and the real card always agreed on.),
        open_expr: "$store.modals.open('unsubscribe-goodbye', {})",
        openable:  true } do %>
    <div class="pointer-events-none w-40 rounded-lg bg-surface border border-subtle shadow p-4 text-center space-y-2">
      <div class="text-2xl leading-none">&#9996;&#65039;</div>
      <span class="block h-2 w-24 mx-auto rounded" style="background: var(--color-text); opacity: .18"></span>
      <span class="block h-1.5 w-28 mx-auto rounded" style="background: var(--color-text); opacity: .18"></span>
      <span class="block h-5 w-full rounded" style="background: var(--color-primary); opacity: .35"></span>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <%= render layout: "style/modal_specimen", locals: {
        label:     "Wallet deposit",
        reference: %(turf-monster's modals/_wallet_deposit — the top-up card raised by the entry-eligibility blocker when a web3 player lacks the USDC or USDT to cover an entry fee. Symmetric to the tokens-picker step inside auth, which is the web2 path, and kept standalone because deposit state has nothing to do with auth credentials. SINGLE-CARD BY DECISION, no step machinery: the shortfall arithmetic, a Pay with Card CTA, and a copy-your-address row for a player who can self-fund from another wallet. THE THREE PROPS ARE THE ARITHMETIC and the card reads all three, so the numbers on screen are the ones the blocker measured; the Layer 2 interpreter does not know the client's balances, so the opener falls back to the session store rather than letting the card show a plausible zero. WHAT THE MIRROR HAD TO FAKE IS THE TELL: it took the wallet address as a fourth prop with a demo default, because a specimen has no session to read. The real card reads $store.session.address, which is why no address is passed here — what you see is this browser's own session. Its Pay with Card is a real Rails-CSRF form to Stripe checkout targeting a new tab, and its Coinbase rail self-gates on the same cdp_ramp? flag as the greyed card above, so on this stack that rail is simply absent.),
        open_expr: "$store.modals.open('wallet-deposit', { neededCents: 500, usdcCents: 120, usdtCents: 0 })",
        openable:  true } do %>
    <div class="pointer-events-none w-40 rounded-lg bg-surface border border-subtle shadow p-4 space-y-2">
      <span class="block h-2 w-24 mx-auto rounded" style="background: var(--color-text); opacity: .18"></span>
      <span class="block h-5 w-full rounded" style="background: var(--color-primary); opacity: .35"></span>
      <span class="block h-5 w-full rounded border border-strong"></span>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <%# THE CALLBACKS ARE DELIBERATELY NOT PASSED. modals/_network_guard reads six
      keys, and two of them, onConfirm and onCancel, are functions the opener in
      app/javascript/solana_utils.js uses to settle a promise. A style-guide
      trigger has no promise to settle, the card guards both with a truthiness
      check, and Cancel and Continue simply close without them — so passing a
      function here would add a brace to the trigger expression and buy nothing.
      Note also what the opener passes and this card must not: an action key,
      which solana_utils uses to BUILD the message string and which the partial
      never reads. %>
  <%= render layout: "style/modal_specimen", locals: {
        label:     "Network guard",
        reference: %(turf-monster's modals/_network_guard — the confirmation shown before a wallet request when the app and the wallet disagree about which network is in play. Raised by confirmSolanaNetworkIntent in app/javascript/solana_utils.js, and only on a real mismatch. APP-SPECIFIC: the engine detects no network and no environment and lends blocks/_card_header alone, so every label here is supplied by this app and only ever DISPLAYED. THE CHECKBOX IS THE POINT and it is not friction for its own sake — a request signed against the wrong network looks identical to a correct one right up until it is irreversible, mainnet money moved on a devnet assumption or the reverse. So the card does three things a plain confirm cannot: it NAMES both the network and the environment, it keeps Continue inert until the person has ticked an explicit acknowledgement, and it gives Cancel equal visual weight rather than burying it. A confirmation whose safe path is harder to reach than its risky one is a formality, and this one is deliberately not. FOUR DISPLAY PROPS ARE PASSED; TWO MORE ARE READ. onConfirm and onCancel are callbacks the opener uses to resolve or reject its promise, and a guide has nothing to settle, so they are left off and both buttons just close. ONLY title CARRIES A DEFAULT in the real card — message, networkLabel and environmentLabel render empty when absent, where the retired mirror defaulted all three and so could never show that.),
        open_expr: "$store.modals.open('network-guard', { title: 'Check Network', message: 'This wallet request is about to use devnet.', networkLabel: 'devnet', environmentLabel: 'QA' })",
        openable:  true } do %>
    <div class="pointer-events-none w-40 rounded-lg bg-surface border border-subtle shadow p-4 text-center space-y-2">
      <%# A BARE EXCLAMATION MARK, not a warning sign. modals/_network_guard
          passes icon_emoji as a plain ! and the retired mirror used a warning
          emoji — a one-glyph drift, and exactly the kind a mini-mock is for. %>
      <div class="text-2xl leading-none font-bold text-heading">!</div>
      <%# the two named facts, then the tick that gates Continue %>
      <span class="block h-1.5 w-full rounded" style="background: var(--color-text); opacity: .3"></span>
      <span class="block h-1.5 w-full rounded" style="background: var(--color-text); opacity: .3"></span>
      <span class="flex items-center gap-1.5">
        <span class="h-3 w-3 rounded border border-strong"></span>
        <span class="flex-1 h-1.5 rounded" style="background: var(--color-text); opacity: .18"></span>
      </span>
      <span class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-1.5">
        <span class="block h-5 rounded border border-strong"></span>
        <span class="block h-5 rounded" style="background: var(--color-primary); opacity: .35"></span>
      </span>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <%# ─── batch 3, the middle five (2026-09-09) ──────────────────────────────
      Five more cards whose engine mirrors drifted by one to six fragments
      each. Their reference prose is transplanted here from
      style/modals/_email_change_pending, _it_begins, _rate_limit_general,
      _newsletter_email and _unsubscribe_confirm. THE ONE UNDISCHARGED HEDGE IN
      THIS FILE: all five were still standing at studio-engine 0.74.4, the
      version the header's batch ledger was measured against. Deleting them is a
      studio-engine task and it comes AFTER, in the order batches 1 and 2 used,
      so nothing is dropped in the gap; discharge it in the HEADER when it
      lands, the way batches 1 and 2 were discharged, rather than re-tensing
      this paragraph. Each drift fragment found on the way is recorded in the
      card it belongs to, so the reason a prop is absent here survives the file
      it was measured against.

      THESE ARE THE IDS THAT COLLIDE with the gem's own Modals section on this
      same page, under the same id string, and every one of them relies on the
      section test filtering registrations down to this app's host. The set is
      NOT enumerated here on purpose — batch 2's preamble enumerated its own and
      was falsified within the day. style_host_section_test derives it from the
      rendered page and names it in its own failure message.

      TWO OF THE FIVE SIT INSIDE THE LAYOUT'S logged_in? BLOCK — newsletter-email
      and unsubscribe-confirm, at layouts/application 1943 and 1961. That is a
      server-side guard of the same shape as the one that greys the Coinbase
      card above, and the difference is that this one is SATISFIED here rather
      than gating the card: the guide is admin-only, so a viewer of this page is
      signed in by construction and both ids are registered on the rendered
      body. Measured against that body, never against the layout source. %>

  <%= render layout: "style/modal_specimen", locals: {
        label:     "Confirm your email change",
        reference: %(turf-monster's modals/_email_change_pending — the handoff after someone changes the email on their own account. AN INFORMATIONAL BEAT, NOT A CELEBRATION, and the distinction is the card's whole job: nothing has changed yet. AccountsController#update HOLDS the change and mails a signed token, and the card's "30 minutes" is the real number rather than a round one — EMAIL_CHANGE_TOKEN_TTL is 30.minutes on that same controller. THE LINK GOES TO THE CURRENT ADDRESS ON PURPOSE, which is why both are printed: the person who did NOT ask for the change is the one who needs to find out, and seeing both is how they notice. APP-SPECIFIC, engine chrome only — it composes blocks/_card_header and nothing else, since the engine sends no confirmation mail and holds no pending change. BOTH PROPS ARE PASSED BECAUSE THE CARD CARRIES NO FALLBACKS: its two spans are bare x-text on props.currentEmail and props.newEmail, so a missing prop renders an EMPTY span where an address belongs. The retired mirror defaulted both to sample addresses, which is precisely the convenience that hides that. Its one production opener is accounts/show.html.erb, replaying the server's flash from an inline script. ONE CONTRAST IN THE MIRROR'S PROSE IS NO LONGER AVAILABLE TO DRAW: it distinguishes this card from a check-email card, and modals/_check_email was deleted in June 2026 when magic-link auth superseded it, so that comparison is left out here rather than repeated.),
        open_expr: "$store.modals.open('email-change-pending', { currentEmail: 'player@turfmonster.media', newEmail: 'new@turfmonster.media' })",
        openable:  true } do %>
    <div class="pointer-events-none w-40 rounded-lg bg-surface border border-subtle shadow p-4 text-center space-y-2">
      <div class="text-2xl leading-none">&#128236;</div>
      <%# both addresses, current above new — the pair is the card %>
      <span class="block h-1.5 w-28 mx-auto rounded" style="background: var(--color-text); opacity: .3"></span>
      <span class="block h-1.5 w-24 mx-auto rounded" style="background: var(--color-text); opacity: .3"></span>
      <span class="block h-5 w-full rounded" style="background: var(--color-primary); opacity: .35"></span>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <%# NO lobbyUrl, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE TRIGGER DECISION. The drain is
      blocks/_cta_redirect, which forks on its destination: truthy and the timer
      runs window.location.href, null and the timer fires a no-op while a click
      falls through to closing the modal. modals/_it_begins wires that
      destination straight to props.lobbyUrl, so here it is the TRIGGER, not the
      card, that picks the fork. Passing one would navigate this guide to a
      contest page eight seconds after the card opened — and go sets redirecting
      true without ever resetting it, so the card would be left a dead spinner on
      the way out. Omitting it is the block's documented gallery mode, and on the
      real card omission is the only way to reach it. %>
  <%= render layout: "style/modal_specimen", locals: {
        label:     "It Begins",
        reference: %(turf-monster's modals/_it_begins — the announcement fired the instant a contest's lock countdown reaches zero. APP-SPECIFIC: the engine owns no contests and no lock. It lends blocks/_cta_redirect, the DRAINING button, which is the whole reason this card is worth a specimen and the one part you have to WATCH rather than read, because a timer is not visible in markup. IT INTERRUPTS SOMEONE MID-PAGE at a moment they did not choose, and the drain is the middle answer: a dismissible modal would leave them on a board that has just gone stale, a hard redirect would take the page away without warning, and the drain announces what is about to happen and gives them the length of the bar to opt out. PRODUCTION RUNS EIGHT SECONDS — the real card passes duration_seconds 8, where the retired mirror shortened it to four so the drain could be watched twice. WHERE THE DESTINATION COMES FROM: contests/_timestamp_countdown opens this id with a lobbyUrl that contests/_contest_header fills from contest_path, so the button reloads the page the viewer is already on into its locked leaderboard view. NOTHING IS PASSED HERE, DELIBERATELY, so the drain plays out and a click closes the card instead of navigating this guide away. The mirror reached that same quiet state a way the real card cannot: it hardcoded a null destination, while this card reads its destination from a prop and so falls quiet only when the prop is absent.),
        open_expr: "$store.modals.open('it-begins', {})",
        openable:  true } do %>
    <div class="pointer-events-none w-40 rounded-lg bg-surface border border-subtle shadow p-4 text-center space-y-2">
      <div class="text-2xl leading-none">&#127967;&#65039;</div>
      <span class="block h-2 w-24 mx-auto rounded" style="background: var(--color-text); opacity: .18"></span>
      <%# the draining CTA, caught partway through its eight seconds %>
      <span class="flex h-5 w-full rounded overflow-hidden">
        <span class="h-5" style="background: var(--color-primary); opacity: .6; width: 40%"></span>
        <span class="flex-1 h-5" style="background: var(--color-primary); opacity: .22"></span>
      </span>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <%= render layout: "style/modal_specimen", locals: {
        label:     "Easy there",
        reference: %(turf-monster's modals/_rate_limit_general — the soft rate-limit wait, opened by the authedFetch 429 interceptor in app/javascript/solana_utils.js when a general-tier cap trips, from hammering Mint USDC say. APP-SPECIFIC, engine chrome only: the engine throttles nothing and lends blocks/_card_header alone. THE TONE IS THE DESIGN. This is a soft cap on someone who did nothing wrong — they clicked too fast — so the card is dismissible, the copy is "Easy there" rather than an error, and it AUTO-CLOSES when the wait elapses instead of making them acknowledge a penalty. A hard-looking card here would punish enthusiasm. THE COUNTDOWN IS THE SERVER'S NUMBER: the interceptor seeds secondsLeft from the 429's own Retry-After. THE CARD READS TWO KEYS AND ITS ONE CALLER PASSES ONE. secondsLeft is what the interceptor sends; message is read with a default of "You are going a bit fast." and NO caller in this app sets it, so that default is the line every real 429 shows — which is why it is left off here rather than filled with copy nobody has met. Ten seconds is a demo value chosen so the auto-close can be watched; above ninety the display switches from seconds to whole minutes. IT COUNTS DOWN ON THE PROPS OBJECT ITSELF, decrementing props.secondsLeft in place where the retired mirror copied the number into local state first, and it clears its interval in a destroy METHOD — Alpine 3 dispatches no destroy DOM event, so a listener would never fire and the timer would outlive every close.),
        open_expr: "$store.modals.open('rate-limit-general', { secondsLeft: 10 })",
        openable:  true } do %>
    <div class="pointer-events-none w-40 rounded-lg bg-surface border border-subtle shadow p-4 text-center space-y-2">
      <div class="text-2xl leading-none">&#9995;</div>
      <span class="block h-1.5 w-28 mx-auto rounded" style="background: var(--color-text); opacity: .3"></span>
      <%# the live number, the only part of this card that moves %>
      <span class="block h-4 w-10 mx-auto rounded" style="background: var(--color-primary); opacity: .45"></span>
      <%# Dismiss is a quiet link rather than a button, because the cap is soft %>
      <span class="block h-1.5 w-16 mx-auto rounded" style="background: var(--color-text); opacity: .18"></span>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <%# THE ONE PROP IS A CALLBACK, so nothing is passed. The card reads exactly
      props.onSubmit, and its submit closes the host FIRST and then calls that
      function with the address — guarded, so with no callback the card simply
      closes. A style guide has no subscribe request waiting on the address, the
      same reason the network guard above is opened without its two settle
      functions. What this trigger gives you is the real capture step ending in a
      real close and no request. %>
  <%= render layout: "style/modal_specimen", locals: {
        label:     "One more thing",
        reference: %(turf-monster's modals/_newsletter_email — the web3 email capture, and the reason it exists is the thing worth understanding: an account signed in with a WALLET has no email on file. Every other newsletter path can assume one; this is the branch that cannot, so it asks — and it asks mid-quest, at the last possible moment, rather than up front where it would read as a signup wall on a product that deliberately has none. ENGINE CHROME PLUS blocks/_card_header AT ITS md SIZE — but do NOT read that as the engine having no newsletter of its own, which is what this line used to claim and what makes this the sharpest card in the section. Measured at studio-engine 0.74.4: the engine defines Studio::Newsletter (lib/studio/newsletter.rb), draws post/delete profile/newsletter at lib/studio.rb:960 and :962, ships blocks/_seeds_bar, and registers a modal under the IDENTICAL id newsletter-email at studio/profiles/_newsletter_modals:40. OWNERSHIP RUNS THE OTHER WAY: the engine's newsletter row opens with "LIFTED FROM turf-monster's /account card" (studio/profiles/_newsletter_section:5) and Studio::Newsletter's two-timestamp state shape is lifted from this app as well. What is genuinely turf's is the SEEDS half — the 25-seed once-ever welcome bonus and the quest it sits in — which is why config/initializers/studio.rb swaps the engine's /profile newsletter row out for this app's. THE ID COLLISION IS REAL AND HARMLESS: the engine's copy answers to $store.profileModals, this one to $store.modals, and profileModals is not on /admin/style at all. IT HANDS THE ADDRESS BACK RATHER THAN POSTING IT — the card closes and calls props.onSubmit, and the caller owns the request, which keeps ONE subscribe path in this app instead of two. Its only opener is the questNewsletter factory in shared/_alpine_factories, and only on that factory's needsEmail branch. THE BUTTON IS NEVER DISABLED, which a reader of the real markup can check and a reader of the retired mirror cannot: the mirror grew a valid getter and bound the button's disabled state to it, while this card leaves the button live and makes submit return early unless the at-sign sits at index one or later. So an invalid address here is a SILENT no-op rather than a greyed button — a real difference in what the person sees, and the mirror taught the friendlier of the two.),
        open_expr: "$store.modals.open('newsletter-email', {})",
        openable:  true } do %>
    <div class="pointer-events-none w-40 rounded-lg bg-surface border border-subtle shadow p-4 text-center space-y-2">
      <div class="text-2xl leading-none">&#128236;</div>
      <span class="block h-1.5 w-28 mx-auto rounded" style="background: var(--color-text); opacity: .3"></span>
      <%# an empty outline, because nothing prefills the field %>
      <span class="block h-5 w-full rounded border border-strong"></span>
      <span class="block h-5 w-full rounded" style="background: var(--color-primary); opacity: .35"></span>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <%# THIS ONE REALLY UNSUBSCRIBES YOU. Every card in this section opens the
      production partial and most of them stop at a close; this card's primary
      button POSTs newsletter_unsubscribe_path for the signed-in admin, fires the
      newsletter-state-changed event the account page listens for, and then swaps
      to the goodbye beat. That is not a defect of the card and not something a
      trigger can soften — the endpoint is server-rendered into its x-data, so
      there is no demo mode to select. It is the cost of showing the card that
      ships; rejoin from the account page afterwards. %>
  <%= render layout: "style/modal_specimen", locals: {
        label:     "Unsubscribe?",
        reference: %(turf-monster's modals/_unsubscribe_confirm — the confirmation before leaving the newsletter, opened from the /account newsletter row and only in its subscribed state. IT LENDS blocks/_card_header ALONE, and the sentence this replaces got the reason wrong twice: the engine owns BOTH a newsletter and an endpoint. Measured at studio-engine 0.74.4, lib/studio.rb:962 draws `delete "profile/newsletter"` to studio/profiles#unsubscribe_newsletter, and studio/profiles/_newsletter_modals registers the engine's own confirmation under the id newsletter-unsubscribe. WHAT IS APP-SPECIFIC IS THIS CARD, not the flow behind it: turf posts to its own newsletter_unsubscribe_path, server-rendered into the x-data, and its id is unsubscribe-confirm — so the two confirmations coexist under different ids on different hosts rather than one shadowing the other. THE QUIET OUT IS THE POINT OF THE CARD. Close is the never-mind, deliberately the understated option while the destructive action wears the full-width button — a confirmation whose safe path is harder to find than its destructive one is a formality rather than a confirmation. THAT BUTTON IS btn-danger, NOT btn-primary, which is where the retired mirror drifted and then described its own drift: the mirror styled it primary and its prose called that the deliberate choice, which was true of the mirror and not of the card it claimed to mirror. On the real card the colour carries the warning, the header takes the X-in-a-red-pill error icon at its lg size rather than the mirror's mailbox emoji, and the subtitle asks "Are you sure?" without the mirror's rejoin-anytime reassurance. IT READS NO PROPS AT ALL — its endpoint is server-rendered into the x-data from newsletter_unsubscribe_path — so this trigger passes none, matching its one production opener, which calls open with an id and no second argument. IT SWAPS TO unsubscribe-goodbye ON SUCCESS, and that card joined this section in the previous batch, so the handoff can now be walked end to end on the real store: a handoff is the one thing you cannot see by looking at either card alone. What the real request adds over the mirror's local resolve is a FAILURE path — an inline error line the mirror had no way to reach.),
        open_expr: "$store.modals.open('unsubscribe-confirm', {})",
        openable:  true } do %>
    <div class="pointer-events-none w-40 rounded-lg bg-surface border border-subtle shadow p-4 text-center space-y-2">
      <%# the X the error icon draws, in its red pill %>
      <div class="text-2xl leading-none font-bold" style="color: var(--color-danger)">&times;</div>
      <span class="block h-2 w-20 mx-auto rounded" style="background: var(--color-text); opacity: .18"></span>
      <span class="block h-1.5 w-28 mx-auto rounded" style="background: var(--color-text); opacity: .3"></span>
      <%# the destructive action, danger-toned, above the quiet never-mind %>
      <span class="block h-5 w-full rounded" style="background: var(--color-danger); opacity: .45"></span>
      <span class="block h-1.5 w-12 mx-auto rounded" style="background: var(--color-text); opacity: .18"></span>
    </div>
  <% end %>

</div>
